      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In construction, dead load refers to the weight of the structure itself, including the materials used, while live load is the weight of temporary or permanent occupants, furniture, and other external forces. For Vancouver, BC, code-compliant post spacing, you'll typically use either the BC Building Code 2020 (Section 4.5.2 and 4.5.3) or the International Residential Code for Britain Columbia (IRBC) or 2018 BC Building Code.\n\n Generally, for typical residential construction in Vancouver, you may use 8 to 16 inches on center (o.c.) for 2x8 framed structures, although this can vary based on local conditions, the type of loads, and building height. I recommend reviewing the relevant code requirements and the American Lumber Standard for Wood Construction (ASLA) to ensure compliance.",
